1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,700
Greetings, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:02,126 --> 00:00:09,504
Today, I will explain something that puzzles many alien visitors: Funny situations, jokes, and laughing - in one word: Humour.

3
00:00:42,747 --> 00:00:48,907
My videos are usually based on a mix of earthling literature, alien sources and conclusions drawn from my own observations.

4
00:00:50,267 --> 00:00:54,402
Humour, however, is a tricky thing which puzzles alien and earthling scientists alike.

5
00:00:54,402 --> 00:01:02,782
So, this episode is entirely based on an earthling book released one solar cycle ago: Inside Jokes - Using Humor to Reverse-Engineer the Mind.

6
00:01:03,043 --> 00:01:06,503
It provides the most convincing theory about humour I've seen so far.

7
00:01:06,923 --> 00:01:08,863
You'll find a link in the description below.

8
00:01:09,423 --> 00:01:18,803
Let's start with a typical funny situation: An earthling crosses a river, a body of hydric acid, by walking on stones, when one of the stones turns out to be an animal.

9
00:01:18,803 --> 00:01:24,436
This mistake triggers a peculiar reaction: The earthling produces strange rhythmic noises, called laughing.

10
00:01:24,698 --> 00:01:26,298
The whole phenomenon is called humour.

11
00:01:27,238 --> 00:01:34,218
Early alien visitors of earth thought that laughing is an artificial respiratory distress induced by the brain, in order to punish mistakes.

12
00:01:34,878 --> 00:01:39,738
However, this can't be right, for earthlings actually enjoy laughing - which is very strange.

13
00:01:40,478 --> 00:01:44,409
Anyway, humour seems to be triggered by mistakes, based on false assumptions.

14
00:01:44,409 --> 00:01:50,229
To understand what is going on, we have first to understand the important role of assumptions for the earthling mind.

15
00:01:51,009 --> 00:01:54,101
Earthlings are often compared to levy drones of the tax authority.

16
00:01:54,670 --> 00:01:59,390
Like drones, earthlings create a model of the environment in their mind, in order to make decisions.

17
00:02:00,030 --> 00:02:01,650
This model is called mental space.

18
00:02:02,183 --> 00:02:06,719
Unlike drones, an Earthling is even capable of handling several mental spaces simultaneously.

19
00:02:06,719 --> 00:02:14,799
For example, one mental space for the present, one for last night, one for the near future, and one for a video game he is currently playing.

20
00:02:15,399 --> 00:02:23,447
However, drones perform continuously full-spectrum scans of the environment, whereas the earthlings' rudimentary senses provide very incomplete data.

21
00:02:23,868 --> 00:02:27,901
In consequence, the earthlings have to fill the gaps by making educated guesses.

22
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:30,006
In other words: Assumptions.

23
00:02:30,647 --> 00:02:32,293
A second problem is time.

24
00:02:32,584 --> 00:02:40,164
No matter whether in an airplane or in a bar, Earthlings have often no choice but to make decisions in a limited amount of time, based on incomplete data.

25
00:02:40,624 --> 00:02:46,041
So, assumptions are unavoidable, but unfortunately, false assumptions can have fatal consequences.

26
00:02:46,823 --> 00:02:48,303
That's where humour comes in.

27
00:02:49,223 --> 00:02:51,143
Let's try to define what humour actually is.

28
00:02:52,023 --> 00:02:57,084
Humour seems to be triggered by the discovery of a false assumption - like the assumption that the thing in the river is a stone.

29
00:02:57,084 --> 00:02:59,245
But not every false assumption is funny.

30
00:02:59,710 --> 00:03:07,970
For example, if the earthling is just sitting at the shore of the river, sees what might or might not be a stone, but doesn't decide what to believe, it's not funny.

31
00:03:08,190 --> 00:03:12,688
He has to commit to the false assumption; he has to take it as truth rather than a mere possibility.

32
00:03:13,518 --> 00:03:21,309
Secondly, if he commits to a false assumption which leads to fatal consequences like losing his legs, it's not funny either - at least not for him, right now.

33
00:03:21,693 --> 00:03:26,047
More generally, it's not funny if the discovery of the mistake is loaded with negative emotions.

34
00:03:26,533 --> 00:03:31,942
So, humour only occurs when the earthling commits to an assumption which turns out to be false, but harmless.

35
00:03:32,766 --> 00:03:40,170
Thirdly, if the earthling wonders consciously whether the thing in the river is an animal, then decides it's a stone, it's not funny either to discover it's an animal.

36
00:03:40,411 --> 00:03:43,144
It's necessary that the assumption has entered the mental space covertly.

37
00:03:43,751 --> 00:03:46,851
Let me explain this in a typically earthling manner: With a metaphor.

38
00:03:47,531 --> 00:03:49,945
Let's say, this is the earthling mind.

39
00:03:50,307 --> 00:03:56,467
Many assumptions enter the mental space by the front-door, are examined and analyzed, before the mind accepts or refuses them.

40
00:03:56,727 --> 00:04:01,567
But some assumptions enter the mind by the backdoor and establish themselves without having been thought over.

41
00:04:02,007 --> 00:04:06,914
Such sneaky assumptions are vicious, if the mind trusts them, as they can do a lot of harm.

42
00:04:06,914 --> 00:04:09,221
Now we can define what humour is.

43
00:04:09,422 --> 00:04:14,042
It's the discovery of those sneaky false backdoor assumptions before they can do any harm.

44
00:04:14,572 --> 00:04:18,697
So, humour occurs when: A) An assumption enters the mental space covertly.

45
00:04:19,364 --> 00:04:21,542
B) The mind takes it to be true.

46
00:04:21,877 --> 00:04:24,209
C) The assumption is revealed as false.

47
00:04:24,452 --> 00:04:28,408
D) The mistake is discovered without causing harm or strong negative emotions.

48
00:04:28,935 --> 00:04:36,522
To be exact, the earthling book this episode is based upon doesn't speak of assumptions, but of active beliefs, which is a bit more precise.

49
00:04:36,982 --> 00:04:41,342
But as often, I had to simplify some things in order to keep the video accessible to anybody.

50
00:04:41,862 --> 00:04:44,985
There are two other species in the galaxy which have a similar sense of humour.

51
00:04:45,755 --> 00:04:47,875
The difference to earthlings is that they hate it.

52
00:04:48,295 --> 00:04:52,866
Both species live in the Aronian system and have been supervised by the same bio administrator.

53
00:04:52,866 --> 00:04:58,766
Apparently, this administrator thought it would be a good idea to have their mind punish them for discovering false assumptions.

54
00:04:59,346 --> 00:05:01,546
The result, however, was a disaster.

55
00:05:02,406 --> 00:05:12,746
One of the species, the Cunctators, became obsessive with double- and triple checking everything before making the smallest decision - even when they were, for example, chased by a horde of furious enemies.

56
00:05:13,226 --> 00:05:18,486
This way they avoided some mistakes, but they became incredibly slow and unable to make decisions under pressure.

57
00:05:19,746 --> 00:05:22,436
Their world eventually became a planet of bureaucrats.

58
00:05:23,178 --> 00:05:28,058
The second species, the Schildarians, became masters of not recognizing errors.

59
00:05:28,458 --> 00:05:34,086
The guildhall of the planetary capital, for example, has no windows because the builders forgot to build them.

60
00:05:34,307 --> 00:05:36,567
But everybody pretends that everything is fine.

61
00:05:36,807 --> 00:05:42,530
The cities of the planet are full of such architectural errors, which makes it a popular destination for tourists.

62
00:05:42,530 --> 00:05:50,850
The earthling humour works much better: Instead of punishing false assumptions, it rewards defusing those sneaky false assumptions before they can do any harm.

63
00:05:51,110 --> 00:05:56,444
The goal is probably to avoid such mistakes in the future without discouraging the earthling from making assumptions.

64
00:05:57,048 --> 00:05:58,728
But what about other people's mistakes?

65
00:05:59,328 --> 00:06:07,012
Well, watching someone else committing to a sneaky false assumption is equally funny - even more if the observer discovers the mistake before the actor falls for it.

66
00:06:07,012 --> 00:06:11,212
It's even funny if the mistake is harmful, as long as the observer doesn't care.

67
00:06:11,792 --> 00:06:17,627
However, death is rarely funny, except for fictional characters or people the observer doesn't care about at all.

68
00:06:17,951 --> 00:06:23,151
That's because earthlings consider death as something tragic, even when someone dies of old age.

69
00:06:23,531 --> 00:06:28,708
This is strange, as earthlings and virtually all creatures on earth are biologically programmed to die some day.

70
00:06:29,796 --> 00:06:31,876
Except for the microbes.

71
00:06:34,756 --> 00:06:42,404
Now we understand why earthlings enjoy humour: It's a reward for defusing false sneaky backdoor assumptions, before they can do any harm.

72
00:06:42,645 --> 00:06:46,465
But that doesn't explain the strange noises earthlings make when encountering humour.

73
00:06:47,145 --> 00:06:48,809
Those noises are called laughing.

74
00:06:49,014 --> 00:06:54,196
To understand this, we have to examine two other things which may trigger laughing: Playing and tickling.

75
00:06:54,196 --> 00:07:01,676
In episode 5 we have learned that earthlings, especially earthling cubs, are used to simulate danger situations for training purposes.

76
00:07:02,056 --> 00:07:03,396
This is called playing.

77
00:07:03,816 --> 00:07:10,762
Now, bystanders may mistake playing for real danger, especially if the players emit alert signals such as screaming or calling for help.

78
00:07:11,182 --> 00:07:14,742
That's why earthling nature developed a false alarm signal: Laughing.

79
00:07:15,184 --> 00:07:22,805
When an earthling hears alert signals mixed with laughing, he understands that the alert is not serious and that the danger only exists in the fictional world of the game.

80
00:07:22,805 --> 00:07:26,305
Odds are that this no danger signal is the original purpose of laughing.

81
00:07:28,305 --> 00:07:31,605
To understand tickling, we need some background information.

82
00:07:31,925 --> 00:07:34,498
It has to do with two feelings: Pain and itching.

83
00:07:34,883 --> 00:07:37,676
Pain is a very unpleasant alert feeling following an injury.

84
00:07:38,003 --> 00:07:41,083
Severe pain usually triggers screaming and calling for help.

85
00:07:41,547 --> 00:07:47,284
Itching is another alert feeling indicating the presence of a parasite on the skin, it triggers scratching to remove the parasite.

86
00:07:47,827 --> 00:07:52,272
Itching is less unpleasant than pain, except when lasting for a prolonged period of time.

87
00:07:52,272 --> 00:07:54,332
But what has this to do with laughing?

88
00:07:54,792 --> 00:08:00,454
Well, serious combat between earthlings, as well as ritualized combat, usually has the goal to inflict pain.

89
00:08:00,697 --> 00:08:08,046
But playful combat, especially between earthling cubs, aims at itching rather than pain, by touching sensitive areas with the fingers.

90
00:08:08,247 --> 00:08:13,623
This is called tickling, and the usual reaction is screaming mixed with laughing, in order not to alert bystanders.

91
00:08:14,428 --> 00:08:18,208
Again, laughter is used as a signal that there is no serious problem.

92
00:08:18,808 --> 00:08:24,392
So initially, laughing is a communication signal, more precisely a signal that indicates false alarm.

93
00:08:24,912 --> 00:08:33,038
Now, a surprise like the animal in the river often triggers screaming before the earthling realizes that there is no real danger, so the earthling laughs to cancel the alarm.

94
00:08:33,038 --> 00:08:36,142
That's possibly the origin of laughing as reaction to humour.

95
00:08:38,486 --> 00:08:39,490
Scientific advice.

96
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:47,070
If you want to examine laughing, it may prove difficult to obtain it with humour, as your test subjects will probably be terrified by the situation.

97
00:08:47,530 --> 00:08:50,259
You might try out other methods like tickling or cannabis.

98
00:08:50,784 --> 00:08:56,884
But the simplest way of making an earthling laugh is by making them inhale nitrous oxide, known on earth as laughing gas.

99
00:08:58,484 --> 00:09:06,011
The alarm signal of screaming is actually infectious: Screaming earthlings may make other earthlings scream, even if they don't know what is going on.

100
00:09:06,411 --> 00:09:10,870
This is called panic, and it's a mechanism to alert a group quickly in case of imminent danger.

101
00:09:11,314 --> 00:09:17,346
Now, if the false alarm signal of laughing has the purpose of cancelling the screaming alarm, it has to propagate in a similar way.

102
00:09:17,648 --> 00:09:20,288
That's probably why laughter is also infectious.

103
00:09:23,608 --> 00:09:24,600
Strategic advice.

104
00:09:25,137 --> 00:09:27,980
When you invade earth, panic can be a powerful weapon.

105
00:09:28,285 --> 00:09:32,595
An effective way of creating panic is to terrorize earthlings with mighty war machines.

106
00:09:33,121 --> 00:09:38,558
However, when you hear earthlings laughing at their approach, your war machines might not be as terrifying as you thought.

107
00:09:38,902 --> 00:09:42,224
One reason might be that you have underestimated the size of earthlings.

108
00:09:46,287 --> 00:09:49,890
As mentioned before, funny situations are rewarded by pleasure.

109
00:09:50,194 --> 00:09:54,718
But earthlings wouldn't be earthlings if they hadn't found ways of creating this pleasure artificially.

110
00:09:55,003 --> 00:09:58,383
In its simplest form, the artificial humour is called jokes.

111
00:09:58,783 --> 00:10:01,871
A joke is typically a funny story, question or remark.

112
00:10:02,301 --> 00:10:05,188
Most jokes feature a fool falling for a false assumption.

113
00:10:05,774 --> 00:10:07,474
Sometimes the fool is in the joke.

114
00:10:08,054 --> 00:10:09,970
Occasionally the teller pretends to be the fool.

115
00:10:10,258 --> 00:10:15,404
But more sophisticated jokes make the listener the fool, by tricking him into making a false assumption.

116
00:10:15,806 --> 00:10:20,778
An example for the fool being inside the joke: How do you get a Polari in a Q3 Mini Saucer?

117
00:10:20,959 --> 00:10:23,390
Tell him 16 of his kind are already inside.

118
00:10:23,840 --> 00:10:26,200
In this case, the Polari is the fool.

119
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:31,595
Of course, the joke isn't funny if you don't know about the Polari culture's obsession with the number 17.

120
00:10:32,116 --> 00:10:39,616
And even if I explain it, it won't get funnier: Explaining a joke is like dissecting an earthling - few people enjoy it, and the earthling dies.

121
00:10:40,376 --> 00:10:44,139
Other examples: How do you get four squids in a Q3 Mini Saucer?

122
00:10:44,485 --> 00:10:47,351
One on the pilot seat, and three on the passenger seats.

123
00:10:47,655 --> 00:10:53,676
In this case, the fool is the teller who pretends not seeing that the problem is the size of the squids, not the number of seats.

124
00:10:53,676 --> 00:10:56,862
How do you get three Andromeda lawyers in a Q3 Mini Saucer?

125
00:10:57,471 --> 00:10:59,811
You can't, because the squids are still inside.

126
00:11:00,291 --> 00:11:08,851
In this case, the listener is the fool, because he was led into assuming that the two jokes are not connected, or in other words, that they don't share the same mental space.

127
00:11:09,071 --> 00:11:11,592
How do you get t(w)o Polaris in a Q3 Mini Saucer?

128
00:11:12,096 --> 00:11:15,463
Via hyperspace express line 37, exit Polaris.

129
00:11:15,864 --> 00:11:18,940
Again, the listener is the fool who misunderstood the question.

130
00:11:18,940 --> 00:11:26,440
This is a pun, a joke that plays with the ambiguity between words - in this case between the plural of Polari, and the star Polaris.

131
00:11:27,180 --> 00:11:33,532
Now some more examples for my earthling viewers, who don't know anything about Polari culture and hyperspace express lines.

132
00:11:33,934 --> 00:11:36,093
How do you know there is a squid in your refrigerator?

133
00:11:36,562 --> 00:11:38,226
By the tentacle traces on the butter.

134
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:41,011
How do you know there are two squids in the refrigerator?

135
00:11:41,011 --> 00:11:43,626
You can hear them giggle when the light goes out.

136
00:11:43,968 --> 00:11:46,165
How do you know there are three squids in the refrigerator?

137
00:11:46,569 --> 00:11:48,229
You can't quite close the door.

138
00:11:48,489 --> 00:11:50,913
How do you know there are four squids in the refrigerator?

139
00:11:51,434 --> 00:11:54,009
There is an empty Q3 Mini Saucer parked outside...

140
00:11:54,632 --> 00:11:56,814
Another form of artificial humour are comedies.

141
00:11:57,242 --> 00:12:00,391
Comedies are full-fledged stories full of jokes and funny situations.

142
00:12:01,346 --> 00:12:06,663
Like all stories, they are created in the form of novels, stage plays, films, etc.

143
00:12:06,663 --> 00:12:11,773
There is an ongoing discussion among alien scientists whether the movie Prometheus is actually a comedy.

144
00:12:12,013 --> 00:12:17,387
On one side it follows the classical comedy formula: Some idiots go some place and do stupid things.

145
00:12:17,668 --> 00:12:20,966
But on the other side, earthlings don't laugh when watching the movie.

146
00:12:21,431 --> 00:12:25,533
My hypothesis is that Prometheus is actually a safety video for future astronauts.

147
00:12:25,915 --> 00:12:31,000
The goal is to warn about stupid mistakes one can make in a space mission: Don't hire morons!

148
00:12:31,402 --> 00:12:33,140
Don't pet creepy alien creatures!

149
00:12:33,761 --> 00:12:35,545
Don't smuggle alien goo on board!

150
00:12:36,046 --> 00:12:38,669
Don't test alien goo on your fellow astronauts!

151
00:12:39,456 --> 00:12:44,662
And, for Galaxy's sake: Don't disturb the siesta of a giant alien who wants to kill all earthlings!!

152
00:12:45,587 --> 00:12:46,707
Tips for tourists.

153
00:12:47,627 --> 00:12:49,896
Usually, earthlings will be afraid when they see you.

154
00:12:50,224 --> 00:12:55,128
But when you visit North America during this time of the year, odds are that you will make earthlings laugh.

155
00:12:55,655 --> 00:13:02,049
The reason is an annual ritualized game where earthling cubs disguise as creatures of the night and grown-ups pretend to be scared by them.

156
00:13:02,049 --> 00:13:07,489
The cubs find this funny, despite the fact that the mistake only occurs in the make-believe world of the game.

157
00:13:08,109 --> 00:13:11,562
Anyway, earthlings will probably think you are a disguised earthling cub.

158
00:13:11,828 --> 00:13:14,453
You might even make new friends and earn some free glucose.

159
00:13:15,856 --> 00:13:18,792
Earthlings usually think of humour as some kind of gratuitous pleasure.

160
00:13:19,116 --> 00:13:27,652
But from an evolutionary point of view, it has a very specific purpose: Training to defuse those nasty false backdoor assumptions, before they can do any harm.

161
00:13:27,652 --> 00:13:33,372
So, once again, it all comes down to our old friend, the genetic imperative.

162
00:13:35,452 --> 00:13:43,852
In the next episode we will learn about earthlings' food: What they eat and drink, how they prepare it, and why they love spoiled food like cheese or wine.

163
00:13:44,552 --> 00:13:47,174
Thanks for watching, and don't forget to be alien!

